## A Structured Ultramicroporous Metal-Organic Framework for Carbon Dioxide Capture†
School of Chemical Engineering and Technology, Xi'an Jiaotong University, Xi'an, Shaanxi 710049, China
Keywords
Metal-organic framework | Shaping | Separation | Carbon capture | Crystal engineering | Microporous materials
## Comprehensive Summary
Carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\right)\) capture is one of the most important aspects of reducing global warming. In terms of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) capture, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have several advantages. However, it isn't easy to shape MOFs while maintaining their performance. Herein, we describe the development of a pellet-shaped ultramicroporous MOF, \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) (3-ain \(=3\) -aminoinoisonicotinic acid), that is capable of selectively adsorbing CO. Polyvinyl butyral (PVB) is used as a binder during the production of \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) MOF pellets. The adequately shaped material can maintain its crystallinity and exhibit a high \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption capacity \((3.73\;\mathsf{m m o l\cdot g}^{-1})\) at ambient conditions, which is significantly greater than those obtained for \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) \((0.63\;\mathsf{m m o l\cdot g}^{-1})\) and CO \((0.90\;\mathsf{m m o l\cdot g}^{-1})\) . Consequently, this material displays high IAST selectivities for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) (26.3, 15/85, V/V) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) (19.2, 1/99, V/V). According to the theoretical calculations, \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) preferentially adsorbs \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) molecules over \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) molecules and CO molecules. The results of experiments on dynamic breakthrough have demonstrated that \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) pellets are capable of effectively separating \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) or \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) mixtures under conditions of dynamic flow. Furthermore, the structured MOF materials can be synthesized in one step at kilogram scale. This work provides an avenue for the shaping of MOFs for potential industrial applications in the future.
## Background and Originality Content
Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS) is considered as the most promising method for reducing carbon emissions and promoting the clean use of fossil fuels.[6] Currently, liquid amines and porous materials decorated with amines based on chemisorption are the most widely used for capturing carbon, which consumes much energy and pollutes the environment.[7-8] Adsorbent-based physical adsorption eliminates these drawbacks and involves low energy consumption and a simple operation process.[9] Pressure swing adsorption (PSA) based on adsorbents has drawn much attention because it operates at a near-ambient temperature compared with cryogenic distillation.[10] Several porous materials have been used to capture \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\).[11] Porous carbon materials and conventional zeolites usually possess lower \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption capacities and selectivity.[12-13] Consequently, CCS requires the invention of more advanced adsorbents.
Porous coordination polymers (PCPs), also named metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), show a large specific surface area, abundant and adjustable pore size, and diverse structures, which make them widely studied in many fields, especially for gas adsorption and separation.[14-22] Various \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) capture and separation methods based on MOFs have been reported.[6,23-25] It is imperative to consider issues such as high-pressure drops, pipe clogging, and dust contamination when using MOFs in the large-scale chemistry industry. It is, therefore, necessary to investigate the feasibility of synthesizing and applying MOFs in potential industrial applications.
The processing and molding of MOF powder are essential to overcome the disadvantages of MOF powder. In order to make MOFs easier to store, transport, and apply, it is essential to process them into different forms while retaining or improving their inherent properties. Processing MOFs into pellets is an effective molding method.[26-27] Ren et al. shaped Zr-MOF powder into spherical pellets with diameters varying between \(0.5\ \mathrm{mm}\) and 15 mm with \(10~\mathsf{w t}\%\) sucrose used as a binding agent. The shaped Zr-MOF was found to lose nearly half of its hydrogen adsorption capacity.[28]
In this work, we reported an ultramicroporous metal-organic framework \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) (3-ain \(=3\) -aminoiso nicotinic acid) with good selective \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption performance. Its \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) separation performances were studied by single adsorption isotherms and breakthrough experiments. In addition, to shape \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) , polyvinyl butyral (PVB) was used as a binder. As a result, the shaped MOF material retains its selective \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption performance with \(90\%\,{\mathsf C O}_{2}\) uptake.
## Results and Discussion
Figure 1a shows a schematic illustration of the preparation procedure for the MOF pellets. Briefly, \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) , polyvinyl butyral (PVB) and ethanol (EtOH) were mixed, and then the paste was shaped into pellets. The framework structure for \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) is shown in Figure 1b, which shows the one-dimensional channels.
We are motivated to further investigate the performance of \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) capture and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) separation due to its distinctive structure and permanent microporosity. Single-component adsorption isotherms were measured for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2},\mathsf{N}_{2},\) and CO at 298 and \(273\;\mathrm{~K~}\) , respectively. As shown in Figure 2a, \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) adsorbs \(83.5\;\mathsf{c m}^{3}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\,\mathsf{C O}_{2},\) which is higher than that for \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) \((14.3\;\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{g}^{-1})\) from 0 to \(100\;\mathsf{k P a}\) . The adsorbed amount of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) is higher than that for most famous MOFs, such as UiO-67 (22.9
\(\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\mathsf{,}\) ),[29] MAF-23 \((56\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})\) ,[30] USTC-253 (47 \(\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})\) , USTC-253-TFA (65 \(\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})\) ,[31] CPM-33a (74 \(\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})\) ,[32] and UiO-66-SO3H-0.15 \((49.3\ c m^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1})\) .[33] In contrast, the \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) uptake is much lower. The results indicate the potential for separating \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) using \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) . The \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption-desorption curves for ten cycles were shown in Figure S3. At \(273\;\mathsf{K},\) the \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) uptakes are determined to be 106.1 and \(24.3\ c m^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}\) , respectively (Figure 2b). As a consequence of this phenomenon, \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption must be physical. Automobile exhaust also contains large amounts of carbon monoxide. The single-component adsorption isotherms for CO at 298 and \(273\;\mathrm{~K~}\) were tested. The amount of CO adsorbed onto \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) is determined to be 31.7 and \(20.3\ c m^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}\) (Figures 2c and 2d), respectively, which is much lower than that for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) . These results show that \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) exhibits great potential for selective \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) capture and a high capture ability.
The ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) was used to evaluate the samples' gas separation abilities. The equimolar IAST selectivity for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) was calculated to be 25.4 at \(298\;\mathsf{K}\) and \(100\;\mathsf{k P a}\) (Figure 3a). For \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) (15/85, V/V), the IAST selectivity was calculated to be 26.3. The value of \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) is higher than that of most promising adsorbents, like BUT-10 (18.6)[29] ZJNU-40 (22.9),[34] PCN-88 (15.2),[35] and HNUST-3 (26.1).[36] In addition, \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) achieves a high selectivity for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) over CO with an IAST selectivity of 16.5 (5/5) and 19.2 (1/99) (Figure 3b) at \(298\;\mathsf{K}\) and 1 bar. These observed selectivities for \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) are higher than those for some reported porous materials such as Mg-MOF-74 (7.5),[37] MIL-101(3.3),[38] HKUST-1 (5.0),[38] and ZJU-HOF-1 (8.7).[39] \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) demonstrates good \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) separation potential due to its high selectivity and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption capacity. As we know, the adsorption enthalpy \((Q_{\mathrm{st}})\) is crucial in explaining the affinity of adsorbent surfaces toward adsorbates and determining the adsorptive selectivity. Based on adsorption isotherms measured at different temperatures, the coverage-dependent enthalpy for adsorption \((Q_{\mathrm{st}})\) was calculated using the virial method. The \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption enthalpy \((31.8\;\mathsf{k J}\!\cdot\!\mathsf{m}\mathsf{o}\mathsf{I}^{-1})\) is much higher than that of \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) \((18.5\;\mathsf{k J}\!\cdot\!\mathsf{m}\mathsf{o}\mathsf{I}^{-1})\) and CO \((18.0\;\mathsf{k J}\!\cdot\!\mathsf{m}\mathsf{o}\mathsf{I}^{-1})\) (Figures 3c and 3d). With such a great difference in isosteric enthalpy, \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) is a great candidate material for separating \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) .
Figure 4 shows the instant adsorption rates for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2},\;\mathsf{N}_{2},\) and CO on \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2},\) illustrating the adsorption kinetics. The experiments were conducted by direct pressurization to \(100\;\mathsf{k P a}\) . In 100 s, \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) almost reaches adsorption saturation. The maximum adsorption rates for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) are determined to range from 3.6 to 7.6
\(\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{s}^{-1}\) (Figure 4a). Nevertheless, the maximum adsorption rates for \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) are determined to range from 0.38 to \(0.86\;\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{s}^{-1}\) (Figure 4b), which are much lower than that for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) . For CO, the maximum adsorption rates on \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) are determined to range from 1.01 to \(2.83\ c m^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{s}^{-1}\) (Figure 4b). Thus, \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) shows excellent potential to separate \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) based on their different adsorption rates. More importantly, the results indicated that the separation could be achieved by combining thermodynamic and kinetic processes.
For comparison of the effect of shaping on the BET surface area of \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2},\,\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms at 77 K were tested for shaped \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) pellets. As shown in Figure 5a, \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) pellets still display type I curves with the microporous structure maintained intact. Nevertheless, the added binder decreases the BET surface area to \(359\ c m^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) . We also measured the adsorption isotherms for \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) pellets at \(298\;\mathsf{K}\) to evaluate the effect of shaping on adsorption performance. The amount of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorbed is determined to be \(64.3\ c m^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) , which is slightly lower than that
for the \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) powder. The uptake of \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and CO also decreased to 12.8 and \(14.2\ c m^{2}.\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) , respectively. By using IAST, the adsorption selectivity for \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{N}}_{2}\) mixtures (15/85, V/V) and \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C O}}\) mixtures (1/99, V/V) at \(298~\mathsf{K}\) and 1 bar was evaluated to investigate the separation performance for \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) pellets. At \(298~\mathsf{K}\) and 1 bar, the IAST selectivity for \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{N}}_{2}\) mixtures (15/85, V/V) was 38.5, which is higher than that for \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) powder. The IAST selectivity for \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C O}}\) mixtures (1/99, V/V) is also increased to 43.7. Although there is a slight decrease in the amount of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorbed, the selectivities for \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{N}}_{2}\) and \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C O}}\) are increased. The results demonstrate the great potential of \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\) pellets for the selective capture of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) from \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{N}}_{2}\) and \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C O}}\) mixtures.
To understand the adsorption mechanism, grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations were performed. As shown in Figure 6a, the \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) molecule was found to interact with hydrogen
atoms through \(\mathsf{O}^{\ldots}\mathsf{H}\) hydrogen bonds with distances of 4.03—4.08 Å. The \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) molecule was found to interact with hydrogen atoms through \(\mathsf{N}^{\ldots}\mathsf{H}\) bonds with distances ranging from 4.19 to 4.23 Å (Figure 6b), which are longer than that for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\). CO is grabbed by an \(\mathsf{O}^{\ldots}\mathsf{H}\) bond with a distance of 4.18 Å (Figure 6c). Based on the above simulation, it can be concluded that \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) has a stronger adsorption affinity than \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and CO, which is in agreement with previous experimental results.
To further evaluate the practical separation performance of \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\), dynamic breakthrough experiments were performed. The flow rate for gas mixtures through the adsorption column was controlled to be \(10\,\mathrm{\mL/min}\). Visible separation of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) can be observed in Figure 7. When equimolar amounts of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) are passed through the column, the \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) breakthrough occurs first. Subsequently, it reaches a plateau, as shown in Figure 7a. \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) then breaks through the adsorbent after a long time. For the breakthrough curves for \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\), a strong roll-up effect is observed. However, when the \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) to \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) ratio is changed to 3/7 (Figure 7b), the roll-up effect in the \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) curve becomes weaker. To evaluate the practical gas separation performance, dynamic breakthrough experiments with \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) mixtures of varying ratios (15/85, 5/95) were also conducted on \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\). As shown in Figures 7c and 7d, a \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) mixture can also be efficiently separated through an adsorption column packed with \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\). When equimolar amounts of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and CO are introduced into the column, the CO gas breaks through the adsorption bed first. Furthermore, breakthrough measurements were conducted for a \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) gas mixture with a ratio of 1/99 (V/V). As shown in Figure 7f, the breakthrough time became significantly longer. The PXRD pattern after breakthrough experiments was shown in Figure S4, which indicated the stability for \(\mathsf{N i}(3\!-\!\mathsf{a i n})_{2}\).
## Conclusions
\(\mathbf{Ni}(3\mathbf{-}\mathbf{ain})_{2}\) is an amino-functionalized ultramicroporous MOF with a pore size of \(5\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) that can be used as a \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) -selective adsorbent for the separation of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) . The ultramicroporous structures interact strongly with \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) molecules and exhibit excellent selectivity for mixtures of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) . According to experiments on adsorption kinetics, \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) has the fastest adsorption rates, leading to the observed separation. Theoretic calculations are used to determine the specific guest-molecule binding sites, which indicate that the framework has the high affinity for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) . Furthermore, dynamic breakthrough experiments conducted at room temperature confirm that \(\mathbf{Ni}(3\mathbf{-}\mathbf{ain})_{2}\) can be effectively used to separate \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) or \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) . Importantly, \(\mathbf{Ni}(3\mathbf{-}\mathbf{ain})_{2}\) can be processed easily into pellets using PVB as a binder while retaining its crystalline structure. Consequently, the MOF pellets exhibit increased \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}\) selectivity. This work provides a strategy for shaping MOF materials that are expected to be utilized in MOF industrial applications.
## Experimental
## Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Ni}(3\mathbf{-}\mathbf{ain})_{2}\)
With a minor modification, \(\mathbf{Ni}(3\mathbf{-}\mathbf{ain})_{2}\) was synthesized according to the method described in the literature.[40] In a \(20~\mathsf{m L}\) autoclave with stainless steel and Teflon lines, the mixture of \(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\) (1.0 mmol, 291 mg), 3-aminoisonicotinic acid \((2.0\;\mathsf{m m o l})\) , \(276~\mathsf{m g})\) , and \(10~\mathsf{m L}\) DMF was sealed. After \(72\,\textrm{h}\) of heating at \(150~^{\circ}\mathsf{C},\) the sample was cooled to room temperature. Using Ni salt as the limiting reagent, the vacuum-dried sample produced \(85.3\%\) of the product. Activated \(\mathbf{Ni}(3\mathbf{-}\mathbf{ain})_{2}\) was obtained by activating the product under vacuum at \(160~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) for \(20\;\mathsf{h}\) .
## Preparation of MOF pellets
First, PVB \(_{(0.5\ g)}\) was dissolved in \(0.5~\mathrm{g}\) of ethanol. The solution was maintained at ambient temperature with stirring for \(^{2\mathrm{~h~}}\) until the components were completely dissolved. Next, \(0.1~\mathrm{g}\) of MOF powder was added to the above solution and stirred at 60 r/min for 15 min. Finally, a semi-dry paste was formed into pellets by extrusion molding.
